{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjYmzikf2ZAEEs3VExa9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016aKmRLHs2hVtvinKeWLTyJ","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Bar 1 of the line has a whole-bar rest, so it is bar 21. Bar 2 has another whole-bar rest, so it is bar 22. Bar 3 is bar 23: an 8th rest, \"rinfor.\" marked above and below the staff, a high 8th (Bb3), a quarter on a ledger line above the staff (C4), then a low quarter (C3). Bar 4 is bar 24: a dotted quarter F3, which is the edition's F3 quarter tied to an 8th. Then \"pia\", a natural before B2, and three beamed 8ths B2, C3, D3 with staccato strokes. Bar 5 is bar 25: two beamed 8ths, Eb3 and C3, with staccato strokes, then quarters F3 and G3. The line ends at the right edge with bar 25 complete. Nothing contradicts the edition. The copyist writes a dotted quarter where the edition ties a quarter to an 8th. A stray note above the staff in bar 3 is ink from the line above.","right":true,"insights":"Two whole-bar rests led into the distinctive \"rinfor.\" bar (rest, a high note, a ledger-line C4, then a low C3), so the start was easy to fix. The \"pia\" bar with its natural on B2 confirmed it. The only real doubt was whether two bars of rest could also match somewhere else in the movement. Seeing the end of the line before this one, or having bar counts written in the manuscript, would have ruled that out."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5245,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5245},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3710df8c-3671-40b0-8990-94e1df368828","uuid":"cc9fa